---
import shared from "../../styles/shared.module.css";
import styles from "./about.module.css";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="About GraphQL Stars"
  description="GraphQL Stars celebrates 52 people each year whose work across the GraphQL GitHub repositories moves the project forward."
>
  <section class:list={[shared.wrap, shared.pageIntro, styles.starsAboutIntro]}>
    <a class={styles.back} href="/stars/">
      ← Meet the GraphQL Stars
    </a>
    <p class={shared.eyebrow}>ABOUT GRAPHQL STARS</p>
    <h1>
      A universe shaped
      <br />
      <em>by contributors.</em>
    </h1>
    <p class={styles.standfirst}>
      GraphQL Stars celebrates 52 people each year whose work across the GraphQL
      GitHub repositories moves the project forward.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class:list={[shared.articleBody, styles.starsAboutCopy]}>
    <h2>GraphQL is a shared effort</h2>

    <p>
      GraphQL does not move forward by itself. Every improvement to the
      specification, its implementations, documentation, tests, tooling, and
      websites begins with someone choosing to contribute their time and
      knowledge.
    </p>

    <p>
      GraphQL Stars celebrates 52 of the people whose work across the GraphQL
      GitHub repositories helped the project move forward. It brings those
      contributors out from behind commits, issues, and pull requests, so the
      wider community can discover the people behind the work.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the Stars began</h2>

    <p>
      Benjie introduced GraphQL Stars at GraphQLConf 2024 during his keynote,{" "}
      <a
        href="https://graphql.org/conf/2024/schedule/5245297ed1f7b82885c742d77f209bda/?name=Keynote%3A%20You%27re%20Our%20Universe%3A%20GraphQL%20Community%20Update%202024"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >
        <cite>You’re Our Universe: GraphQL Community Update 2024</cite>
      </a>
      . Looking back over a year of work across the project, he celebrated some
      of the people improving GraphQL and invited others to help shape what
      comes next.
    </p>

    <p>
      That celebration became GraphQL Stars: a new class of contributors,
      recognised each year.
    </p>
  </div>
  <section class={shared.wrap} aria-labelledby="talk-heading">
    <div class={styles.starsVideoFrame}>
      <iframe
        src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/bCBZTNib0mA?si=yxipoNUAP8qZsM3v&amp;start=203"
        title="You're Our Universe: GraphQL Community Update 2024"
        loading="lazy"
        allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
        referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
        allowfullscreen
      ></iframe>
    </div>
    <div class={styles.starsVideoCaption}>
      <div>
        <h2 id="talk-heading">
          You're Our Universe: GraphQL Community Update 2024
        </h2>
        <p>Benjie Gillam · Community Gardener · GraphQLConf 2024</p>
      </div>
      <a
        href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bCBZTNib0mA"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >
        Watch on YouTube ↗
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class:list={[shared.articleBody, styles.starsAboutCopy]}>
    <h2>52 Stars, every year</h2>

    <p>
      Each class covers contributions made from September through August. The
      class of 2026, for example, reflects work carried out between September
      2025 and August 2026.
    </p>

    <p>
      There are 52 Stars in each class: one for every card in a deck. Explore
      the class, choose a card, and meet someone helping to build GraphQL.
    </p>

    <a class={shared.button} href="/stars/2026/">
      Explore the class of 2026 <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </a>
  </div>
</Layout>
